comments:
@s-hertel  and @bcoca: Thanks for the excellent explanation of the behavior. Your example highlights why I suggested looking at the name + content of the handler vs the name + entry point.  Deduplication of handlers is something I was relying on, and without knowing all these nuances of in-memory/runtime roles vs the on disk representation was frustrating.

I dig the import_tasks/handler definition in the playbook, but that does break encapsulation which is why I was using roles in the first place.  If I have to define the handlers in some root handlers/ directory, but the role they're being called from is in roles/service/elasticsearch/instance/tasks/main.yaml, it's not as clean as I'd like.

I keep thinking, "maybe just add an option to the tasks block to denote this is a static handler", but I don't like that. It opens the door for an issue where someone uses a signature variable in the handler and accidentally sets the `static: true` element.  I still think fingerprinting handlers by name + content would make all the instances you mention work and all the pain I'm having disappear.
